//! SQL over Chronos, with our own parser and executor. Tables are the same `table/id` JSON rows the
//! rest of Chronos uses, so SQL, the shell, the HTTP API, search and branches all see the same data.
//!
//! A table made with CREATE TABLE has a schema (typed columns, NOT NULL, DEFAULT, a primary key)
//! kept as a row of `_sonos_tables`, and its rows are keyed by their primary key. A table made by
//! writing JSON rows has no schema: SQL sees its key as the text column `id` plus every field any
//! of its rows has.
//!
//! So far: CREATE TABLE, DROP TABLE, INSERT, SELECT on one table (WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, OFFSET),
//! UPDATE, DELETE, RETURNING and $1 parameters. Each statement is atomic.
//! ponytail: WHERE on anything but the primary key scans the table; use the search indexes for
//! equality filters when tables get big.

